### Opening

Every terminal has that driver who just *knows* the stops.

He knows which entrance actually works for a 28-, 40-, or 48-foot trailer. He knows receiving is around the back even though the address points to the front office. He knows you have to call a specific number before you back in. He knows the tight corner, the low wires, and the place where the pavement drops off.

Then he quits, gets transferred, or moves to a different route.

That knowledge usually leaves with him. The next driver shows up with the same address and starts from zero.

### What actually gets lost

The useful details are rarely written down anywhere official:

- Which gate or driveway to use
- Whether the truck will fit
- If backing is required and how tight it is
- Where receiving or the dock actually is
- Any check-in steps or quirks that save time (or prevent problems)

Standard navigation apps get you to the street address. Company systems usually stop at the same address. The practical knowledge that makes the delivery smooth lives in the heads of the drivers who have already done it.

### Why this keeps happening

Routes change. People leave. New drivers get assigned. Temporary coverage happens. The experienced driver isn’t always there to give a quick verbal brief.

Most of the time the industry treats this as just part of the job. FreightIQ treats it as a solvable problem.

### What FreightIQ does differently

FreightIQ lets drivers capture the important details while they are still fresh — usually just the few things that actually matter at that stop.

The next driver (or the same driver weeks later) can see that information before they arrive. Over time, the useful knowledge builds up instead of disappearing every time a route changes hands.

You don’t have to write a long report. One clear detail is often enough to help the next person.

### Closing

Driver knowledge should not disappear when routes change.

